In JavaScript, you can make an HTTP request using the `XmlHttpRequest` object or the `fetch` API. Here are examples of both:

**Using `XmlHttpRequest`**
```javascript
var xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
xhr.open('GET', 'https://www.example.com', true);
xhr.onload = function() {
  if (xhr.status === 200) {
    console.log(xhr.responseText);
  }
};
xhr.send();
```
* `new XMLHttpRequest()` creates a new instance of the `XmlHttpRequest` object.
* `open()` sets the request method, URL, and whether the request should be asynchronous (in this case, `true`).
* `onload` sets a callback function that will be called when the request is complete.
* `send()` sends the request.

**Using `fetch` API**
```javascript
fetch('https://www.example.com')
  .then(response => response.text())
  .then(data => console.log(data));
```
* `fetch()` makes a GET request to the specified URL.
* `then()` sets a callback function that will be called when the response is received.
* `response.text()` returns the response body as a string.
* `console.log()` logs the response data to the console.

You can also specify headers, method, and other options using the `fetch` API:
```javascript
fetch('https://www.example.com', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {
    'Content-Type': 'application/json'
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({ foo: 'bar' })
})
  .then(response => response.json())
  .then(data => console.log(data));
```
* `method` specifies the request method (in this case, `POST`).
* `headers` specifies the request headers.
* `body` specifies the request body as a JSON string.

Both of these examples make a GET request to a URL. You can modify the request method, URL, and other options to suit your needs.
